bolt3 — third-party attribution
=======================

⛔ KIND CHANGED 2026-09-09, from `provenance note` to `third-party
attribution`. This file carries a CONDITION now, and the reason is that it
always did: `src/root.zig` embeds BOLT#3's Appendix D and Appendix E test vectors verbatim from `lightning/bolts`, and that repository is
licensed CC-BY 4.0 — which asks for attribution. Redistributing this module —
alone, or as part of zig-libs — must keep this file with it.

⛔⛔ THE REPOSITORY WAS DISTRIBUTING TWO OPPOSITE ANSWERS ABOUT ONE UPSTREAM.
`modules/lnwire/NOTICE`, `modules/lninvoice/NOTICE` and `modules/k256/NOTICE`
all record `lightning/bolts` as CC-BY 4.0 and attribute it. This file, and its
siblings `sphinx` and `bolt8`, said the opposite — that BOLT text is "not a
copyrightable work (merger doctrine)" and needs no entry — while quoting that
same repository and vendoring its test vectors. Two incompatible answers about
one rightsholder, shipped together.


Kept as a module-local NOTICE (same placement as the sibling `bolt8`/`sphinx`/
`bip340`/`taproot`/`musig2` modules, which are likewise self-contained
crypto/codec modules not folded into the root `NOTICE`).

Provenance
----------

bolt3 — clean-room implementation from the Lightning BOLT#3 specification
("Bitcoin Transaction and Script Formats", lightning/bolts), specifically the
"Key Derivation" section (per-commitment and revocation public/secret keys)
and Appendix D ("Per-commitment Secret Requirements"). Written from the spec
text and its normative test vectors; no reference implementation was copied.

The elliptic-curve group (secp256k1) and SHA-256 come from Zig's standard
library (`std.crypto.ecc.Secp256k1`, `std.crypto.hash.sha2.Sha256`). The
scalar-reduction idiom for hash-to-scalar mirrors this repository's own
`bip340` module.

Verification vectors are the BOLT#3 Appendix D (generation) and Appendix E
(key derivation) test vectors from the lightning/bolts repository, reproduced
byte-exact in the module's tests — which is precisely why this file now carries
a condition rather than being a record only. "Reproduced byte-exact" and "needs
no attribution entry" cannot both be true of a CC-BY upstream.

